#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acf91a0-d644-5837-be02-ae7a75a73521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287284} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdefb26-6f9c-59e6-a300-b9ba36b0662e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287285} {:answer/id #uuid "414a4834-aebd-5766-a02f-e494225cf0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287286} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4b52f4-4953-5c88-bd2d-1636592e7089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287287} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d3a771-a99f-5f99-8677-9f9f7ab0151c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdefb26-6f9c-59e6-a300-b9ba36b0662e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287285} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c5d9901-45ec-5a82-b5ff-6ae652bb348e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe4502b9-4c15-5777-ace8-7177e53571cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-106398" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/7b9f67594871385ccf7ac02c3af9e47a585116c162a08cdc1795d452a09912fe.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-3)$ with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-3),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "861da652-f677-5fa7-a40e-7ee0a493bbb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287291} {:answer/id #uuid "1f83c84e-621b-5000-b60c-556777725315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287292} {:answer/id #uuid "df45a23e-d530-5666-a794-d534c05bcf4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287293} {:answer/id #uuid "ba900f3f-81f4-5508-8e8f-09127ffabd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287294} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7df34a-a9ab-5f79-95ae-9b8e42fb067d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f83c84e-621b-5000-b60c-556777725315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287292} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc3cf9b-cb13-50c7-b8c9-940fb76a2b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7003b4fc-eeae-5c86-9a52-755b32646d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-106400" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/8a04ad6bb8ce108e4f63fddcd5c178ec855a646a14761ad989a9e6c9328ddbd0.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6db43ff41344f292c8cb5805febd175088fe068fe89927bade0c6fc2246c8781.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "698cb1f5-9f40-502e-9c6c-d125f336a37d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309943} {:answer/id #uuid "334bb100-678c-551c-b431-5ad508a48042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309944} {:answer/id #uuid "d1619ef5-bb2e-58c9-8361-e4c22b35dd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309945} {:answer/id #uuid "1f096eca-93a6-5e7a-a73c-8a6d3e69b7a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309946} {:answer/id #uuid "994a8f87-54d3-56eb-a26c-246d8d55a67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "334bb100-678c-551c-b431-5ad508a48042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309944} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6788439-9f18-5be1-81dc-e9244ab289ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db2471c-43eb-5703-bc68-40fdaaff995e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106406" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad88058-c875-5036-b4ea-3628069695fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb03b3c-e66b-5556-847f-39b8e859dc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287334} {:answer/id #uuid "432d428a-efcb-5ece-9091-69f2afa8dec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287335} {:answer/id #uuid "3c92de15-0997-5844-af75-b5727a650d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287336} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f59f1d-6c3d-54ad-867d-b18963ce5dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad88058-c875-5036-b4ea-3628069695fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad1b808d-be7f-533e-807d-3c989cce7046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86ea583d-a580-5671-b8bf-22431844e7be" :question/math-academy-id "q-15843" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287340} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e675469-7ed3-50ca-b8c4-63856f23fd96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "775dbfe2-7347-5fb3-9641-24c4c41065e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775dbfe2-7347-5fb3-9641-24c4c41065e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287342} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c750959-1bdb-5f24-93a8-046ad41f914d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8dcce0e-e879-55c5-84f2-41534e0d01fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-274855" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/d906e74fdd78e074ab410d1042c134c892fd1b50b78b0f50a76ccf2ee203c203.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24554dd2-c7a7-5795-ace8-ef8ee8123e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287345} {:answer/id #uuid "5a761898-639e-58fc-b2fd-c7718ff280db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24554dd2-c7a7-5795-ace8-ef8ee8123e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287345} :answer-field/id #uuid "2071a2f6-650b-5c57-86b4-17df74f93151" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66173e3f-74f3-53c0-ad15-c0b28d4d1d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sin(\\theta)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66173e3f-74f3-53c0-ad15-c0b28d4d1d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sin(\\theta)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287348} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6c69d3-7405-5114-a936-18767e95e99d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca5b2e3e-ddb1-5792-98f6-dd4e9edd115c" :question/math-academy-id "q-327171" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,5).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(0,5),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13dc05ed-4d2a-54f7-9143-1df7dfaf56b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13dc05ed-4d2a-54f7-9143-1df7dfaf56b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309969} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68d4dad-6dbc-5905-9104-5cbbef20ce2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a17ad85-75b3-57c6-af87-8cba05167eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-327808" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "582cfdb3-9e7d-5f15-a26a-d00ec9f67751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287370} {:answer/id #uuid "cd12cacf-3474-5a8a-8a58-48b6696f9204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287371} {:answer/id #uuid "69ea148e-dfc2-5dd4-bbfe-d1130acc22ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287372} {:answer/id #uuid "3a98dff9-85fe-5c45-bae9-9e80401d71b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287373} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a87621-cfad-513c-8bc6-4e1fddf2fcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a87621-cfad-513c-8bc6-4e1fddf2fcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287374} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9984a4-cf5d-59d7-aabc-48c476f6a41a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99d45658-64c1-527b-ba91-3ee0b91560a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-6880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/86dcfa13b7863c08b348855e776a6df709ecac499af02c2479cfdf484eeaf328.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b591b959-0fa6-5a9f-9c27-2d53c19f3ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150577} {:answer/id #uuid "d6492c8b-6cd1-5aa0-99f1-a1b0cdabe68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150578} {:answer/id #uuid "feb74112-2dcd-5dc5-9b05-85e93ac4fe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150579} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4b28d0-0159-54f3-bdb2-0d6d0d566c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150580} {:answer/id #uuid "69563d64-d0ff-507a-b25a-bf11edff79ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\fra